  data: {
    LLM: 'Game: Spizzichino. Logic: See rules.objective and rules.gameplay.',
    Player: "Spizzichino (or 'Pizzichino') is a complex two-player Italian card game played with a 40-card deck (Italian suits). It combines elements of Briscola (trick-taking) and Scopa (capture/fishing) with meld-making (similar to Bezique/Pinochle). The name likely refers to 'picking' or 'nibbling' at points in small increments. The game is highly strategic and lesser-known outside Italy.",
    objective: 'Score more points than your opponent by winning tricks (card points plus last-trick bonus) and declaring buongiochi (three or four of a rank, or Napoletana 3-2-A of a suit). The game ends when a player reaches 51 or more points.',
    gameplay: 'Two players play 20 tricks. Non-dealer leads first; winner of each trick leads the next. You may play from your hand or the top of one of your two face-up packets (revealing the next card). Must follow suit when possible. Same suit: higher card wins; different suit: lead wins. Honours (3, 2, Ace) on top of a packet may be moved to hand. Declare buongiochi (three or four of same rank, or Napoletana 3-2-A of a suit) when acquired to score 3 or 4 points. After 20 tricks, count card points in tricks won plus 1 for last trick; add meld points. Game ends when a player reaches 51 points.',
    keyRules: [
      'Must follow suit when possible.',
      'Same suit: higher card wins; different suit: lead wins.',
      'Declare buongiochi when acquired (three/four of rank or Napoletana 3-2-A).',
      'Game ends when a player reaches 51 points.',
    ],
    bonusRuleGuids: [],
    useTrump: false,
    trumpBonusValues: null,
    setup: {
      players: 'Two players; they take turns to deal. Each player has a hand of 10 cards and two face-up packets of 5 cards on the table (only the top card of each packet is visible). Play is from hand or from the top of a packet.',
      deck: '40 cards (Italian or Stripped 52).',
      equipment: '40-card Italian pack (Latin suits). In some regions a 40-card French pack with Queen replacing Cavallo.',
      dealing: 'Dealer shuffles and creates eight packets of 5 cards face down. Opponent chooses four packets: two placed face-up on the table (top card visible only), two picked up as a 10-card hand. Dealer does the same with the other four. Each player has a 10-card hand and two face-up packets of 5 cards.',
    },
    turnFlow: 'Two players play 20 tricks. Non-dealer leads first; winner of each trick leads the next. You may play from your hand or the top of one of your two face-up packets (revealing the next card). Must follow suit when possible. Same suit: higher card wins; different suit: lead wins. Honours (3, 2, Ace) on top of a packet may be moved to hand. Declare buongiochi (three or four of same rank, or Napoletana 3-2-A of a suit) when acquired to score 3 or 4 points. After 20 tricks, count card points in tricks won plus 1 for last trick; add meld points. Game ends when a player reaches 51 points.',
